\section*{Conceptual Division Between Two Sub-Fields}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{\textcolor{red}{Long-run growth}}: Study the determinants of trend growth.

    \item \textbf{\textcolor{red}{Short-run business cycles}}: Study deviations from trend.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Business cycles}: Short-term fluctuations of output around its long term level     (also called potential or natural output).  \textcolor{red}{Most recessions are brief}. Instead, the time to return to the previous peak level or the previous trend path may be quite extended. 


\item Why do we care? \textcolor{red}{Unemployment} is highly countercyclical; \textcolor{red}{consumption} and investment are highly procyclical; \textcolor{red}{inflation} is moderately procyclical; \textcolor{red}{policy} may play a role in mitigating negative effects.

\end{itemize}

\textbf{Three generations:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Generation 0: \textcolor{red}{Large-Scale Macroeconometric Models}
\item First generation: basic \textcolor{red}{Real Business Cycle} models
        \item Second generation: \textcolor{red}{New Keynesian} models
        \item Third generation: \textcolor{red}{Heterogeneous-Agent} models
        \item + \textcolor{red}{Search and Matching} models
    \end{itemize}

1950s to 1970s

Core Structure:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Large reduced-form systems of \textcolor{red}{aggregate variables} estimated on \textcolor{red}{historical time-series}. 
\end{itemize}\\

Critiques: 
Terrible performance in 1970s:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Failed to forecast \textcolor{red}{stagflation} (stagnation and inflation).
        \item Subject to influential critiques by leading macroeconomists, including Lucas (1976): \noindent One of the most important \textcolor{red}{paradigm shifts} in modern \textcolor{red}{macroeconomic theory}. Policy evaluation cannot rely on non-\textcolor{red}{structural} relationships estimated from historical data, since parameters are not \textcolor{red}{invariant to policy}. Agents reoptimize and change their behaviour when policy changes. Models must instead be \textcolor{red}{micro-founded}: built on deep \textcolor{red}{primitive parameters} (preferences, technology, constraints) that are plausibly \textcolor{red}{invariant to policy}.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Main objective:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Can \textcolor{red}{technology shocks} account for fluctuations in key aggregates (output, consumption, investment, hours, $\ldots$)?*
    \end{itemize}\\



\textbf{Core Structure}: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{Micro-founded, optimization-based} dynamic models
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Built from primitives:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Preferences, technology, and constraints (\textcolor{blue}{deep parameters})
            \item \textcolor{blue}{Equilibrium} concept
        \end{itemize}
        \item In equilibrium:
        \begin{itemize}
            \item Agents think \textcolor{blue}{intertemporally}
            \item \textcolor{blue}{Individual decisions} on consumption, labor supply, and investment choices jointly satisfy budget and resource constraints
        \end{itemize}
    \end{itemize}\\

Canonical setup:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Representative agent
        \item Few frictions (if any)
        \item Small number of shocks, mainly \textcolor{red}{technology shocks}
    \end{itemize}

\end{itemize}\\


\textbf{Model evaluation}:
\begin{itemize}
        \item Compare \textcolor{red}{data moments} and \textcolor{red}{model generated moments} (standard deviations, autocorrelations, cross-correlations; the 'KP table')
        \item Pioneered ``\textcolor{red}{calibration}'' to discipline parameters, but models were too simple to tightly link new mechanisms to micro-level data

    \end{itemize}\\

\textbf{Pros:}
- Models capture some aggregate comovement
- RBC models \textcolor{red}{addressed the Lucas critique} using micro-founded, optimization-based frameworks.



\textbf{Limitations} within this framework:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Too little volatility in hours worked
        \item Weak internal propagation

    \end{itemize}\\

Rationale: they \textcolor{red}{abstracted} from \textcolor{red}{key features of actual economies}:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{blue}{Nominal rigidities and monetary non-neutrality}
    \item \textcolor{blue}{Other frictions} (e.g., labor market and financial frictions)
    \item \textcolor{blue}{Heterogeneity} across households and firms
\end{itemize}\\

As a result:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{blue}{Limited role for stabilization policy}, as fluctuations are largely efficient
    \item Difficulty explaining \textcolor{blue}{deep and persistent recessions}
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Core structure:} 
1980s: New Keynesian paradigm aimed to microfound Keynesian concepts:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Inefficient fluctuations
        \item Nominal price and wage stickiness
        \item Monetary non-neutrality
    \end{itemize}


\textbf{Limitations:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Largely static
        \item Designed mainly for qualitative analysis
    \end{itemize}

\textbf{1.Timeline:}
 Early New Keynesian models:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Largely static
        \item Designed mainly for qualitative analysis
    \end{itemize}\\

 In parallel, RBC models:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Quantitative and micro-founded
        \item Abstracted from monetary factors and other frictions
        \item Late 1990s: \textcolor{red}{natural synthesis} of \textcolor{red}{New Keynesian} and \textcolor{red}{RBC} approaches
\end{itemize}\\


\textbf{2. Motivating Evidence:}
\textcolor{blue}{Monetary policy has real effects (see note attached)}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Volcker disinflation and early 1980s recession
        \item VAR evidence
    \end{itemize}
\textcolor{blue}{Panel data evidence on price/wage stickiness}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Prices fixed for about 9 months on average
        \item Wages adjusted roughly once a year
    \end{itemize}
\textcolor{blue}{Inefficient employment fluctuations}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Countercyclical labor wedge
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{3. Core Structure}: 
    \begin{itemize}
        \item DSGE framework (building on RBC models)
        \item Imperfect competition
        \item Nominal rigidities
    \end{itemize}\\

\textcolor{blue}{Baseline representation:} a \textcolor{red}{three-equation model}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item IS equation (aggregate demand)
        \item NK Phillips curve (aggregate supply)
        \item Interest rate rule (monetary policy)
    \end{itemize}\\


\textbf{4. New insights:}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Monetary transmission depends on expectations of future policy
    \item Natural (flexible-price) output and interest rate are key policy benchmarks
    \item Flexible-price equilibrium corresponds to an RBC model
\end{itemize}\\


\textbf{5. Limitations:}
New Keynesian models typically retain a \textcolor{red}{representative household}. Introducing heterogeneity allows us to address two broad questions:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{From macro to micro:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item What are the \textcolor{blue}{distributional effects} of aggregate shocks and policies?
        \item \textit{Does expansionary monetary policy benefit poorer hhs more than richer ones?}
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textcolor{red}{From micro to macro:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Do \textcolor{blue}{distributional features} affect aggregate shocks and policy transmission?
        \item \textit{Do liquidity-constrained households alter the aggregate response to policy?}
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}\\


Consider that: 
\begin{itemize}
    \item A model can speak to distributional effects (\textcolor{red}{from macro to micro}) only if it explicitly incorporates the relevant dimensions of heterogeneity.

    \item Whether heterogeneity matters for aggregate outcomes (\textcolor{red}{from micro to macro}) is a quantitative question: eg In some cases, distributional features have limited effects on aggregates, and a simpler \textcolor{red}{representative-agent} model may be sufficient (meaning: from micro to macro, you understad that even without too much micro heterogeneity you can understand the macro phenomenon).
\end{itemize}


From micro to macro, case study: 

The GR exposed limits of \textcolor{red}{representative-agent} business-cycle models.

The crisis originated in housing and credit markets:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Falling house prices had \textcolor{red}{heterogeneous effects} across households, depending on their \textcolor{red}{balance-sheet composition}.
    \item The pass-through from wealth losses to consumption varied with \textcolor{red}{liquidity access} and \textcolor{red}{marginal propensities to consume (MPCs)}.
    \item The collapse in demand and lending generated \textcolor{red}{uneven employment losses} across \textcolor{red}{occupations} and \textcolor{red}{skill groups}.
\end{itemize}\\

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{HANK Models: Framework and Key Ingredients}}\\


\begin{itemize}
    \item Integrates \textcolor{red}{Heterogeneous Agents (HA)} and \textcolor{red}{New Keynesian (NK)} features.

    \item Combines \textcolor{red}{HA}, \textcolor{red}{nominal rigidities}, and \textcolor{red}{aggregate shocks}, allowing analysis of:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textcolor{blue}{Aggregate fluctuations}
        \item \textcolor{blue}{Distributional effects}
        \item \textcolor{blue}{Stabilization policies}
    \end{itemize}

    \item Main focus on household heterogeneity; key elements include:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textcolor{blue}{Idiosyncratic risk:} mainly exogenous to labor income.
        \item \textcolor{blue}{Imperfect insurance:} a limited set of assets and borrowing constraints.
        \item \textcolor{blue}{Heterogeneous consumption decisions:} more complex IS relation, but the NK Phillips curve and interest rate rule remain largely unchanged.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}


\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{HANK Models: Key Takeaways}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{Heterogeneity changes the transmission of aggregate shocks and policies.}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Idiosyncratic risk and imperfect insurance generate \textcolor{blue}{heterogeneous MPCs out of income and wealth}.
        \item Shocks and policies are amplified or dampened through their effects on the \textcolor{blue}{income and wealth distribution}.
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{New insights for macroeconomic analysis.}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Driving forces and transmission channels can be identified by studying how shocks and policies affect heterogeneous households.
    \end{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{Distributional and normative implications.}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Important consequences for both monetary and fiscal policy.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}



\textbf{Current issues and directions for future research:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Computational complexity and solution methods.
        \item Endogenous unemployment risk; optimal policy design; integration of household and firm heterogeneity; interactions with networks; and other emerging topics.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textbf{Modern business cycle theory:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Focuses on \textcolor{red}{Dynamic Stochastic General Equilibrium (DSGE)} models
        \item Micro-founded, optimization-based models of economic dynamics
        \item Match key aggregate fluctuations in the data
        \item Key mechanisms disciplined by micro-level evidence
    \end{itemize}


\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Search and Matching Models: Motivation}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Baseline RBC, NK, and HANK models:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Employment fluctuates at the \textcolor{red}{intensive margin} (hours per worker).
        \item \textcolor{red}{No unemployment}, strictly speaking.
        \item Unable to explain \textcolor{red}{unemployment fluctuations}, which arise from \textcolor{red}{worker flows}.
    \end{itemize}

    \item Modeling unemployment dynamics:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Requires an explicit \textcolor{red}{flow-based} labor market framework.
        \item The workhorse framework is the \textcolor{red}{search and matching model}.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}


\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Search and Matching Models: Core Structure and Mechanisms}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Labor markets are characterized by \textcolor{red}{frictions}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Time-consuming search
        \item Costly vacancy posting and hiring
        \item Unemployment coexists with vacancies
    \end{itemize}

    \item Core elements:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item A \textcolor{blue}{matching function} mapping unemployed workers and vacancies into matches
        \item Job finding and separation rates
        \item Wage determination through \textcolor{blue}{bargaining}
    \end{itemize}

    \item Key mechanisms for \textcolor{red}{labor market fluctuations}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Vacancy posting respond to productivity, demand, and policy shocks
        \item Unemployment dynamics are driven by job finding and separation rates
        \item Labor market policies as \textcolor{red}{stabilization tools} (e.g.\ \textcolor{red}{unemployment insurance})
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}


\textcolor{red}{Key contributions to macroeconomic models:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Introduction of new data and concepts (unemployment, vacancies, flows).
        \item Search behavior as a source of new mechanisms and shocks.
        \item Match-specific rents and wage-setting processes.
        \item Interaction of labor market institutions, frictions and stabilization policy.
    \end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Specifying a Model I}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item To describe an economy, one needs to specify:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item \textcolor{red}{Commodities:} what is consumed, produced, and traded.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Preferences:} what commodities households like or dislike.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Endowments:} exogenously given quantities of commodities.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Technology:} how firms transform commodities (inputs) into other commodities (outputs).
        \item \textcolor{red}{Policies:} policy instruments controlled by the government (taxes, etc.).
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\bigskip


\begin{itemize}
    \item A model typically features (at most) three types of agents. For each, we must specify decisions and constraints:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item \textcolor{red}{Households:} choose commodities to max preferences, subject to a constraint set that specifies which combination of commodities they can choose from.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Firms:} choose inputs to max (expected) profits, subject to their production plans being technologically feasible.
        \item \textcolor{red}{Government:}
        \begin{itemize}
            \item \textcolor{blue}{Positive analysis:} policies taken as given, s.t.\ a budget constraint.
            \item \textcolor{blue}{Normative analysis:} policies chosen to max an objective, s.t.\ a budget constraint.
        \end{itemize}
    \end{enumerate}

    \item To obtain predictions, we must also specify:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item An \textcolor{red}{equilibrium concept}, describing how agents interact.
        \item The \textcolor{red}{information structure} available to agents when making decisions.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\section*{1. What Defines the RBC World?}
An RBC model has:
\begin{itemize}
    \item A representative household maximizing her utility
    \item Firms maximizing profits
    \item Perfect competition
    \item Flexible prices and wages
    \item Complete markets (or at least no relevant financial frictions)
    \item Rational expectations
    \item No nominal rigidities
\end{itemize}

\textbf{Crucially:}

\begin{center}
All prices adjust instantly.
\end{center}

\section*{2. What Does Monetary Policy Actually Change?}
Monetary policy changes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Nominal interest rate
    \item Money supply
    \item Inflation
\end{itemize}

These are nominal variables.

The question is:

\begin{center}
Can changing nominal variables affect real allocations 
$(C, L, Y, K)$?
\end{center}

\section*{3. The Key Mechanism: Relative Prices Determine Real Decisions}

In RBC:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Households care about real wages.
    \item Firms care about real marginal costs.
    \item Intertemporal choices depend on the real interest rate.
\end{itemize}

All decisions depend on:

\begin{center}
Relative prices, not absolute price levels.
\end{center}

If the central bank doubles the money supply:
\begin{itemize}
    \item All prices double.
    \item All wages double.
    \item The nominal interest rate adjusts via the Fisher equation.
\end{itemize}

But:
\begin{align*}
\text{Real wage} &= \text{unchanged}, \\
\text{Real interest rate} &= \text{unchanged}, \\
\text{Marginal productivity conditions} &= \text{unchanged}.
\end{align*}

So:

\begin{center}
Real allocations don’t change.
\end{center}

This is \textbf{classical neutrality}.

\section*{4. Why Does This Happen?}

Because there is no friction preventing prices from adjusting.

If the central bank injects money:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Aggregate demand rises.
    \item Firms immediately raise prices.
    \item Wages adjust.
    \item Real wage stays the same.
    \item Labor supply stays the same.
\end{itemize}

No real distortion ever appears.

Money is just a unit of account.

\section*{5. Intuition: The ``Veil of Money''}

In RBC, money is a veil.

Imagine rewriting the whole economy in euros instead of dollars.

Nothing real changes.

In RBC, a monetary expansion is like changing units from meters to centimeters.

\begin{itemize}
    \item Nominal values change.
    \item Real tradeoffs do not.
\end{itemize}

\section*{6. When Would Money Matter?}

Money matters only if something prevents instantaneous adjustment:
\begin{itemize}
    \item Sticky prices
    \item Sticky wages
    \item Information frictions
    \item Financial constraints
    \item Segmented markets
\end{itemize}

Without these frictions, there is no channel.

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{We Start with the Neoclassical Growth Model. Why?}}


- Microfounded, \textcolor{red}{ancestor} of modern DSGE models
\begin{itemize}

   \item Useful to introduce \textcolor{red}{solution methods}
    \item Clarifies the link between \textcolor{red}{social planner allocations} and the decentralized \textcolor{red}{CE}
\end{itemize}

Agents are endowed with labor and capital and rent these out to firms. 

{\Large\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Representative Firm’s Problem}}}

\begin{itemize}
  \item Constant returns to scale: 
\end{itemize}
\[
\max_{k_t, n_t} \; F(k_t,n_t) - r_t k_t - w_t n_t
, \qquad 
k_t, n_t \ge 0
\]
\begin{itemize}
  \item The firm’s problem is \textcolor{red}{static}:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item The firm does not own capital.The firm does not make intertemporal decisions.
    \item Inputs are rented each period.
  \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}

\vspace{1em}

{\Large\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Representative Household’s Problem}}}
\vspace{1em}
\begin{itemize}
  \item Since all households are identical, the economy admits a \textcolor{red}{representative household}.
  \item Given prices $\{w_t, r_t\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$, the representative household solves
\end{itemize}
\[
\max_{\{c_t,i_t,x_{t+1},k_t,n_t\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}
\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t u(c_t)
\qquad \text{s.t.}
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
c_t + i_t &= r_t k_t + w_t n_t + \pi_t && \forall t, \\
x_{t+1} &= (1-\delta)x_t + i_t && \forall t, \\
n_t &= 1,\; k_t = x_t && \forall t \text{ (Inada)}, \\
x_0 &\text{ given.}
\end{aligned}
\right.
\]
x is the total k is what you use!

A \textcolor{red}{competitive equilibrium} consists of:

- Prices $\{w_t, r_t\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$, household allocations $\{c_t, i_t, x_{t+1}, k_t^{s}, n_t^{s}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$, firm allocations $\{y_t, k_t^{d}, n_t^{d}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}$, such that:
\begin{enumerate}
  \item Given prices, the household allocation solves the \textcolor{red}{household’s problem}.

  \item Given prices, the firm allocation solves the \textcolor{red}{firm’s problem}.

  \item \textcolor{red}{Markets clear} for all $t$:
\end{enumerate}

\[
y_t = c_t + i_t 
\qquad \text{(goods market)}
, \quad 
n_t^{d} = n_t^{s} 
\qquad \text{(labor services)}
, \quad 
k_t^{d} = k_t^{s} 
\qquad \text{(capital services)}
\]

Recall: 
\[
\max_{\{c_t,k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}
\sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t u(c_t) \qquad \text{s.t.} \quad 
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
c_t + k_{t+1} - (1-\delta)k_t &= r_t k_t + w_t \qquad \forall t, \\
c_t, k_{t+1} &\ge 0 \qquad \forall t, \\
k_0 &\text{ given}
\end{aligned}
\right.
\]

The household’s state variables each period are:
  \begin{itemize}
    \item its own capital stock $k$,
    \item the aggregate capital stock $K$, which determines factor prices $w(K)$ and $r(K)$ <- households are price takers <- can think at this as "they inherit from otpimal past aggreagte decisions some K, or they know the efficient eq K that will be generated"
  \end{itemize}
Recall state variables: it summarizes everything inherited from the past (Markov) and determines current feasible choices.

Control variables: $(c,k')$. 
-

\begin{itemize}
  \item Given $w(K)$, $r(K)$, and $H(K)$, the household’s Bellman equation is
\end{itemize}

\[
V(k,K)
=
\max_{c,k'}
\left\{
u(c) + \beta V(k',K')
\right\}
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad
\left\{
\begin{aligned}
c + k' &= (1 + r(K) - \delta)k + w(K), \\
K' &= H(K).
\end{aligned}
\right.
\]

\section*{Basic Representation of Uncertainty: Events and Event Histories}

\begin{itemize}
    \item In each period $t$, a stochastic event $s_t$ is realized.

    \item $s_t \in S = \{\eta_1, \eta_2, \ldots, \eta_N\}$, where $S$ is the set of possible events.

    \item We assume:
    \begin{enumerate}
        \item $S$ is finite,
        \item $S$ does not depend on time.
    \end{enumerate}

    \item If no confusion arises, we write $s_t = 1$ instead of $s_t = \eta_1$.

    \item Example: $S$ represent weather states, with $s_t = 1$ indicating rain and $s_t = 2$ sunshine.

    \item An event history up to time $t$ is
    \[
        s^t = (s_0, s_1, \ldots, s_t) \in 
        \underbrace{S \times \cdots \times S}_{t+1\text{-fold product}}
        = S^t,
    \]
    where $S^t$ denotes the set of possible event histories.
\end{itemize}


\section*{Basic Representation of Uncertainty: Probabilities}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The probability of an event history $s^t$ is denoted by
    \[
        \pi(s^t).
    \]

    \item The conditional probability of next period’s event is
    \[
        \pi(s_{t+1} \mid s^t).
    \]
\end{itemize}


\section*{Basic Representation of Uncertainty: Commodities}

\begin{itemize}
    \item Commodities are indexed by time $t$ and by event histories $s^t$:
    \[
        c_t(s^t).
    \]

    \item An umbrella if it rains is a different good from an umbrella if it is sunny.

    \item More generally, consumption at time $t$ after a particular history $s^t$ is distinct from consumption at time $t$ after any other history.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Model 2: Stochastic NGM --- Adds Uncertainty, No Labor Disutility}}

\textcolor{red}{What changes vs.\ Model 1:} productivity $A_t(s^t)$ is stochastic; allocations are contingent on history $s^t$; the objective becomes an expected discounted sum. $n_t = 1$ still (no labor disutility).

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 0: Setup.}} Choices: $\{c_t(s^t),\, k_{t+1}(s^t)\}$. States: $k_t(s^{t-1})$, $s^t$.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 1: Problem.}}
\[
\max_{\{c_t(s^t),\, k_{t+1}(s^t)\}}
\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}
\sum_{s^t \in S^t}
\beta^t \pi(s^t)\,
u\!\big(c_t(s^t)\big)
\quad
\text{s.t.} \quad
c_t(s^t) + k_{t+1}(s^t) = A_t(s^t)\, f\!\big(k_t(s^{t-1})\big) + (1-\delta)\, k_t(s^{t-1}),
\quad k_0,\, s_0 \text{ given.}
\]
Resource constraint is an equality (Inada; same convention as Model 1).

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 2: Lagrangian.}}
\[
\mathcal{L}
=
\sum_{t=0}^{\infty}
\sum_{s^t}
\beta^t \pi(s^t)
\Big[
u\!\big(c_t(s^t)\big)
+
\lambda_t(s^t)
\Big(
A_t(s^t) f\!\big(k_t\big) + (1-\delta) k_t - c_t(s^t) - k_{t+1}(s^t)
\Big)
\Big].
\]
\textcolor{red}{Convention:} $\lambda_t(s^t)$ is the \emph{current-value} multiplier, sitting inside $\beta^t \pi(s^t)$, exactly as $\lambda_t$ sat inside $\beta^t$ in Model 1.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 3: FOCs.}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] w.r.t.\ $c_t(s^t)$: \quad $u'\!\big(c_t(s^t)\big) = \lambda_t(s^t)$
    \item[(ii)] w.r.t.\ $k_{t+1}(s^t)$: \quad $\lambda_t(s^t) = \beta \sum_{s_{t+1}} \pi(s_{t+1}\mid s_t)\, \lambda_{t+1}(s^{t+1}) \Big[A_{t+1}(s^{t+1}) f'\!\big(k_{t+1}(s^t)\big) + 1-\delta\Big]$
\end{itemize}

Remark: $s^{t+1}\mid s^t$ sums over all next-period realizations $s_{t+1}$ extending the current history $s^t$.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 4: Euler equation.}} Substitute (i) into (ii):
\[
\boxed{u'(c_t) = \beta\, \mathbb{E}_t \Big\{ u'(c_{t+1}) \big[A_{t+1} f'(k_{t+1}) + 1-\delta\big] \Big\}}
\]
Same structure as Model 1 but with $\mathbb{E}_t\{\cdot\}$ wrapping the RHS (rational expectations: agents know the stochastic process and form model-consistent forecasts).

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 5: Boundary conditions.}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $k_0$, $s_0$ given.
    \item TVC: $\lim_{t\to\infty} \beta^t\, \mathbb{E}_0\big[u'(c_t)\, k_{t+1}\big] = 0$.
\end{itemize}

\section*{State and Control Variables}

The planner’s problem has:

\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{State variables}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $k$ (endogenous state),
        \item $A$ and $s$ (exogenous states).
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textcolor{red}{Control variables}:
    \begin{itemize}
        \item $c$,
        \item $k'$.
    \end{itemize}
\end{itemize}



\bigskip
\section*{Recursive Formulation of Planner’s Problem}
\begin{itemize}
    \item The planner’s Bellman equation is:
\end{itemize}

\[
V(k,A,s)
=
\max_{c,k'}
\left\{
u(c)
+
\beta
\sum_{s'}
V(k',A',s')\, \pi(s'\mid s)
\right\}
\quad 
\text{s.t.}
\quad
\begin{cases}
c + k' = A s f(k) + (1-\delta)k, \\
A' = A s, \\
c,\, k' \ge 0.
\end{cases}
\]

\begin{itemize}
    \item The solution consists of two policy functions:
\end{itemize}

\[
c = C(k,A,s),
\qquad
k' = G(k,A,s).
\]

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Real Business Cycle Model: the intensive margin! }}

\begin{itemize}
    \item The central mechanism is \textcolor{orange}{intertemporal substitution of leisure.}
    \item The basic RBC model focuses on the \textcolor{orange}{intensive margin} (changes in hours worked per worker), rather than the \textcolor{blue}{extensive margin} (changes in employment).
    \item All individuals are employed (the number of employed workers is fixed) but continuously adjust the number of hours they work. But, since agents are identical, each supplies the same number of hours.
\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}

    \item The basic RBC model departs from NGM by including disutility from labot: 
    \[
    u_t \equiv u(c_t, n_t)
    \]
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Functional Form Specification}}

\begin{itemize}
    \item A common parametric specification is:

    \[
    u(c_t, n_t) = \frac{c_t^{1-\sigma} - 1}{1 - \sigma} - \frac{n_t^{1+\phi}}{1 + \phi}
    \quad \text{for } \sigma \neq 1
    \]

    \[
    u(c_t, n_t) = \log c_t - \frac{n_t^{1+\phi}}{1 + \phi}
    \quad \text{as } \sigma \to 1
    \]

    \item $\sigma$ and $\phi$ control curvature of utility and disutility

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Model 3: RBC --- Adds Labor Disutility to Model 2}}

\textcolor{red}{What changes vs.\ Model 2:} utility is $u(c_t, n_t)$ with labor disutility; labor $n_t$ becomes a choice variable. Everything else (stochastic $A_t$, Inada/equality convention, current-value multiplier $\lambda_t$ inside $\beta^t$) is unchanged.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 0: Setup.}} Choices: $\{c_t, n_t, k_{t+1}\}$. States: $k_t$, $A_t$.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 1: Problem.}}
\[
\max_{\{c_t,n_t,k_{t+1}\}_{t=0}^{\infty}}
\mathbb{E}_0 \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t u(c_t,n_t)
\quad
\text{s.t.} \quad
c_t + k_{t+1} = A_t F(k_t,n_t) + (1-\delta)k_t,
\quad k_0 \text{ given.}
\]

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 2: Lagrangian.}}
\[
\mathcal{L}
=
\mathbb{E}_0 \sum_{t=0}^{\infty}
\beta^t
\Big[
u(c_t,n_t)
+
\lambda_t
\big(
A_t F(k_t,n_t) + (1-\delta)k_t - c_t - k_{t+1}
\big)
\Big]
\]
Same convention: $\lambda_t$ is the current-value multiplier, inside $\beta^t$.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 3: FOCs.}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] w.r.t.\ $c_t$: \quad $u_c(c_t,n_t) = \lambda_t$
    \item[(ii)] w.r.t.\ $n_t$: \quad $-u_n(c_t,n_t) = \lambda_t\, A_t F_n(k_t,n_t)$ \quad \textcolor{red}{(new: labor FOC)}
    \item[(iii)] w.r.t.\ $k_{t+1}$: \quad $\lambda_t = \beta\, \mathbb{E}_t \big\{ \lambda_{t+1} [A_{t+1} F_k(k_{t+1},n_{t+1}) + 1-\delta] \big\}$
\end{itemize}

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 4: Optimality conditions.}} Substitute (i) into (ii) and (iii):

\textcolor{orange}{Intratemporal (labor supply, from i+ii):}
\[
\boxed{-u_n(c_t,n_t) = u_c(c_t,n_t)\, A_t F_n(k_t,n_t)}
\]
Marginal disutility of labor $=$ marginal product of labor $\times$ marginal utility of consumption.

\textcolor{blue}{Intertemporal (Euler, from i+iii):}
\[
\boxed{u_c(c_t,n_t) = \beta\, \mathbb{E}_t \Big\{ u_c(c_{t+1},n_{t+1}) \big[A_{t+1} F_k(k_{t+1},n_{t+1}) + 1-\delta\big] \Big\}}
\]
Same Euler as Model 2, but $u_c$ now depends on $(c_t, n_t)$ because utility includes labor.

\medskip
\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 5: Boundary conditions.}} Same as Model 2: $k_0$ given, TVC $\lim_{t\to\infty} \beta^t\, \mathbb{E}_0[u_c(c_t,n_t)\, k_{t+1}] = 0$.

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Decentralized CE for Model 3 (RBC): Same Template}}

\textbf{Goal:} Show that household + firm optimization under competitive markets reproduces the planner's allocation (Model 3).

\medskip
{\Large\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Firm's Problem (static)}}}

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 1:}} $\max_{k_t,n_t}  A_t F(k_t,n_t) - r^k_t k_t - w_t n_t$.

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 3 (FOCs):}} $\textcolor{orange}{w_t} = A_t F_n(k_t,n_t)$, \quad $\textcolor{orange}{r^k_t} = A_t F_k(k_t,n_t)$.

\bigskip
{\Large\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Household's Problem}}}

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 1: Problem.}}
\[
\max_{\{c_t,n_t,k_{t+1}\}} \mathbb{E}_0 \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t u(c_t,n_t)
\quad \text{s.t.} \quad
c_t + k_{t+1} = (r^k_t + 1 - \delta)k_t + w_t n_t, \quad k_0 \text{ given.}
\]

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 2: Lagrangian.}}
\[
\mathcal{L} = \mathbb{E}_0 \sum_{t=0}^{\infty} \beta^t \Big[ u(c_t,n_t) + \lambda_t \big((r^k_t+1-\delta)k_t + w_t n_t - c_t - k_{t+1}\big) \Big]
\]
Same convention: $\lambda_t$ current-value, inside $\beta^t$.

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 3: FOCs.}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item[(i)] w.r.t.\ $c_t$: \quad $u_c(c_t,n_t) = \lambda_t$
    \item[(ii)] w.r.t.\ $n_t$: \quad $-u_n(c_t,n_t) = \lambda_t\, \textcolor{orange}{w_t}$
    \item[(iii)] w.r.t.\ $k_{t+1}$: \quad $\lambda_t = \beta\, \mathbb{E}_t\big\{ \lambda_{t+1} (\textcolor{orange}{r^k_{t+1}} + 1-\delta) \big\}$
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{orange}{Step 4: Substitute prices.}} Using firm FOCs ($w_t = A_t F_n$, $r^k_t = A_t F_k$) and (i):

\textcolor{orange}{Intratemporal:} $-u_n(c_t,n_t) = u_c(c_t,n_t)\, A_t F_n(k_t,n_t)$ \quad $\checkmark$ identical to planner (Model 3).

\textcolor{blue}{Intertemporal:} $u_c(c_t,n_t) = \beta\, \mathbb{E}_t\big\{ u_c(c_{t+1},n_{t+1}) [A_{t+1} F_k(k_{t+1},n_{t+1}) + 1-\delta] \big\}$ \quad $\checkmark$ identical.

\textcolor{red}{Conclusion:} competitive prices decentralize the planner's allocation.

\bigskip
\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Interpretation of Prices}}
\begin{itemize}
    \item $\textcolor{blue}{w_t}$: intratemporal price of leisure (cost of working one more hour in consumption units).
    \item $\textcolor{orange}{r_{t+1}} \equiv r^k_{t+1} + 1 - \delta$: gross return on savings = intertemporal price of consumption.
    \item Intertemporal price of leisure: $\frac{\textcolor{teal}{w_t}}{\textcolor{teal}{w_{t+1}}} \textcolor{orange}{r_{t+1}}$. If wages rise or returns rise, households substitute toward working today.
\end{itemize}

\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Labor Supply and the Frisch Elasticity}}

\begin{itemize}

    \item Consider the parametric utility specification:

    \[
    u(c_t,n_t)
    =
    \log c_t
    -
    \frac{n_t^{1+\phi}}{1+\phi}
    \]

    \item Intratemporal labor supply condition (same as Model 3: $-u_n = u_c \cdot w_t$):

    \[
    -u_n(c_t,n_t)
    =
    u_c(c_t,n_t)\, w_t
    \quad
    \Rightarrow
    \quad
    w_t = c_t n_t^{\phi}
    \]

This is the labor supply equation. It tells you: For a given consumption level, How wages must relate to hours worked. We now compute the elasticity of this labor supply. 


    \item The \textcolor{orange}{Frisch elasticity of labor supply} measures the percentage change in hours worked in response to a one-percent change in the wage, \emph{holding consumption constant}:

    \[
    \left.
    \frac{d \ln n_t}{d \ln w_t}
    \right|_{c_t}
    =
    \frac{1}{\phi}
    \]

    \item A lower $\phi$ implies a higher labor supply elasticity.

\end{itemize}

\medskip 


\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Intertemporal Substitution of Leisure}}

\begin{itemize}

    \item \textbf{Step 1: Deterministic Euler equation.}

    Euler: 

    \[
    u_c(c_t,n_t)
    =
    \beta r_{t+1}\, u_c(c_{t+1},n_{t+1})
    \]

    where $r_{t+1}$ is the gross return on savings.

    \item With log utility, $u_c = 1/c_t$. Substituting:

    \[
    \frac{1}{c_t}
    =
    \beta r_{t+1}
    \frac{1}{c_{t+1}}
    \quad
    \Rightarrow
    \quad
    \frac{c_{t+1}}{c_t}
    =
    \beta r_{t+1}
    \]

    Thus, consumption growth depends on patience ($\beta$) and the return on savings ($r_{t+1}$).

    \item \textbf{Step 2: Intratemporal labor supply condition.}

    From the labor FOC:

    \[
    w_t = c_t n_t^{\phi}
    \]

    \item \textbf{Step 3: Combine across time.}

    Using $c_{t+1}/c_t = \beta r_{t+1}$ and the labor supply equation at $t$ and $t+1$:

    \[
    \frac{w_{t+1}}{w_t}
    =
    \frac{c_{t+1}}{c_t}
    \left(
    \frac{n_{t+1}}{n_t}
    \right)^{\phi}
    \]

    Substitute consumption growth:

    \[
    \frac{w_{t+1}}{w_t}
    =
    \beta r_{t+1}
    \left(
    \frac{n_{t+1}}{n_t}
    \right)^{\phi}
    \]

    Taking logs:

    \[
    \ln \frac{n_{t+1}}{n_t}
    =
    \frac{1}{\phi}
    \left(
    \ln \frac{w_{t+1}}{w_t}
    -
    \ln r_{t+1}
    -
    \ln \beta
    \right)
    \]

    \item \textbf{Interpretation:}

    \begin{itemize}
        \item If wages are expected to rise, households work more tomorrow than today.

        \item If the return on savings is high, households work more today, save, and consume (and enjoy leisure) tomorrow.

        \item These adjustments reflect \textcolor{orange}{intertemporal substitution of leisure}, whose magnitude is governed by the Frisch elasticity $\frac{1}{\phi}$.
    \end{itemize}

\end{itemize}


\textbf{\textcolor{blue}{Income and Substitution Effects}}

\begin{itemize}

    \item Consider a temporary increase in the real wage $w_t$.

    \item \textcolor{orange}{Substitution effect:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Leisure becomes more expensive relative to consumption.
        \item Households substitute away from leisure toward work.
        \item Labor supply increases.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textcolor{blue}{Income effect:}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item Higher wages raise lifetime income.
        \item If leisure is a normal good, households demand more leisure.
        \item Labor supply decreases.
    \end{itemize}

    \item The net response reflects the balance of these two effects.

    \item In the RBC model, technology shocks change  \textcolor{orange}{intertemporal relative prices} (wages and returns).  Fluctuations mostly arise from optimal  \textcolor{orange}{intertemporal substitution} (see: how technology shocks drive wages in RBC models).

    \item \textcolor{orange}{Frisch elasticity} isolates the substitution effect 
    (holding marginal utility of wealth constant).

\end{itemize}

\begin{itemize}

    \item In the RBC model, technology shocks drive fluctuations in wages, and thus intertemporal substitution of labor.

    \item \textcolor{orange}{Frisch Elasticity and Amplification}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The mechanism requires a sufficiently large \textcolor{orange}{Frisch elasticity}.
        \item Micro evidence suggests relatively small values (typically below 1).
        \item With a small elasticity, labor supply responds weakly to wage changes.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textcolor{orange}{Unemployment}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item The basic RBC model has no unemployment.
        \item It cannot account for the level or cyclical fluctuations of unemployment.
    \end{itemize}

    \item \textcolor{orange}{Extensive vs.\ Intensive Margin}
    \begin{itemize}
        \item In the data, most variation in labor input comes from changes in employment, not hours per worker.
        \item The basic RBC model focuses only on the intensive margin.
    \end{itemize}

    \item These limitations motivate models with indivisible labor.

\end{itemize}

Why studying the planner problem?
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textcolor{red}{Characterize efficiency:} in frictionless environments, the competitive equilibrium coincides with the planner’s allocation.

    \item \textcolor{red}{Assess policy:} deviations from the planner’s solution reveal inefficiencies and the potential scope for policy.

    \item \textcolor{red}{Simplify analysis:} the planner’s problem abstracts from prices and is typically easier to solve.
\end{itemize}

{\Large\textcolor{blue}{\textbf{Real Interest Rate and User Cost of Capital}}}

What is the net return from investing in capital (real interest rate)?
\[
R_{t+1} = r_{t+1} - \delta.
\]

\begin{itemize}
  \item Rearranging,
\end{itemize}

\[
r_{t+1} = R_{t+1} + \delta.
\]

\begin{itemize}
  \item Thus, the rental rate must compensate investors for both the \textcolor{red}{opportunity cost of funds} (the real interest rate) and \textcolor{red}{depreciation}. Their sum is the \textcolor{red}{user cost of capital}.
\end{itemize}
